  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1133 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nLow impact weather is likely through the extended period. Another\nseasonably chilly night is forecast Monday night with lows again \nin the 50s areawide (isolated upper 40s far NE OK and NW AR). By \nTuesday, high pressure slides eastward, bringing winds out of the \nsouth again. At the same time, modest upper level ridging works \ninto the region, with a weakness in heights developing over Texas.\nThis pattern will provide warmer temperatures Tuesday onward with\nhighs in the 80s. A shortwave moving through the upper midwest \nwill send a weakening cold front toward our area mid-week, but \nlatest model guidance keeps this front just north of the FA with \nlittle or no impacts expected at this time.\n\nGuidance indicates lower pressure will drift northeast out of \nTexas by this weekend, which may result in some low rain potential\nfor the area, primarily in far E OK and NW AR. The evolution of \nthis system remains uncertain, however, and plenty of dry ensemble\nmembers reduce confidence in the rain potential. For now, a dry \nforecast remains appropriate and PoPs were held to 10 percent or \nlower. Looking further ahead, guidance indicates a stronger storm \nsystem and associated cold front may impact the region early-mid \nnext week. Model variability increases dramatically by that time, \nbut if it pans out, colder temperatures and higher rain chances \nwould be expected. Time will tell.",
